06 June 2026\n\n--- Fragmento 3 (Fonte: 10. Cardiovascular Disease and Risk Management: Standards of Care in Diabetes—2024.pdf, Pág: 5) ---\nable for individuals with diabetes and\nmildly elevated blood pressure (systolic\n>120 mmHg or diastolic>80 mmHg) and\nshould be initiated along with pharmaco-\nlogic therapy when hypertension is diag-\nnosed (Fig. 10.2) (53). A lifestyle therapy\nplan should be developed in collaboration\nwith the person with diabetes and dis-\ncussed as part of diabetes management.\nUse of internet or mobile-based digital\nplatforms to reinforce healthy behaviors\nmay be considered as a component of\ncare, as these interventions have been\nfound to enhance the efﬁcacy of medical\ntherapy for hypertension (54,55).\nPharmacologic Interventions\nRecommendations\n10.7 Individuals with conﬁrmed ofﬁce-\nbased blood pressure$130/80 mmHg\nqualify for initiation and titration of\npharmacologic therapy to achieve the\nrecommended blood pressure goal of\n<130/80 mmHg. A\n10.8 Individuals with conﬁrmed ofﬁce-\nbased blood pressure$150/90 mmHg\nshould, in addition to lifestyle therapy,\nhave prompt initiation and timely titra-\n\n	Based on the provided clinical guidelines, the best hypertension treatment for patients who are also diabetic depends on their initial blood pressure and the presence or absence of albuminuria or coronary artery disease (CAD).\n\nFor patients with an initial blood pressure of less than 130/80 mmHg and no albuminuria or CAD, lifestyle management should be initiated along with diabetes management. Lifestyle interventions may include weight loss, dietary modifications, physical activity, and stress management. If blood pressure control is not achieved with lifestyle therapy alone, a single-pill antihypertensive combination may be added.\n\nFor patients with an initial blood pressure of 130/80 mmHg or higher, or those with albuminuria or CAD, pharmacologic therapy should be initiated promptly. The recommended first-line therapy is an ACEi or ARB in combination with a CCB or diuretic. The goal is to achieve blood pressure control while minimizing adverse effects. If target blood pressure is not achieved with two agents, a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist may be considered, or the patient may be referred to a specialist with expertise in blood pressure management.\n\nIn all cases, a lifestyle therapy plan should be developed in collaboration with the patient, and digital health interventions may be used to reinforce healthy behaviors and enhance the effectiveness of medical therapy.\n\nIt's important to note that these guidelines are based on the updated standards of care in diabetes and may change as new research and clinical evidence emerge.	2026-06-06 21:17:56.938004
