nloaded from http://diabetesjournals.org/care/article-pdf/47/Supplement_1/S158/759373/dc24s009.pdf by guest on 06 June 2026\n\n--- Fragmento 3 (Fonte: 2023 AHA-ACC-ACCP-ASPC-NLA-PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease: A Report of the American Heart Association - American College of Cardiology Joint Committee on Clinical Practice Guidelines.pdf, Pág: 54) ---\nand goals, and include shared decision-making \nbetween the clinicians and the patients. For exam-\nple, there may be patients who may prefer revascu-\nlarization even if not on GDMT.\nDownloaded from http://ahajournals.org by on June 6, 2026\n\n	Urinalysis and urine culture are two distinct diagnostic tests used in the evaluation of urinary system diseases.\n\nUrinalysis is a laboratory test used to examine the physical and chemical properties of urine, such as color, odor, pH, specific gravity, protein, glucose, and the presence of red and white blood cells, bacteria, and other microscopic elements. It provides valuable information about kidney function, urinary tract infection, diabetes, and other conditions.\n\nUrine culture, on the other hand, is a laboratory test used to identify and isolate microorganisms present in urine to determine the cause of a suspected urinary tract infection (UTI). The test involves inoculating a sterile growth medium with urine and incubating it for a specific period to allow bacterial growth. The bacteria are then identified by their morphology and growth characteristics.\n\nIn summary, urinalysis provides information on urine composition and potential health issues, while urine culture specifically identifies and quantifies microorganisms present in urine to determine the cause of a UTI. Both tests are essential for the diagnosis and management of urinary system diseases.	2026-06-06 21:19:12.798699
