wnloaded from http://diabetesjournals.org/care/article-pdf/47/Supplement_1/S158/759373/dc24s009.pdf by guest on 06 June 2026\n\n--- Fragmento 3 (Fonte: 2023 AHA-ACC-ACCP-ASPC-NLA-PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease: A Report of the American Heart Association - American College of Cardiology Joint Committee on Clinical Practice Guidelines.pdf, Pág: 54) ---\nand goals, and include shared decision-making \nbetween the clinicians and the patients. For exam-\nple, there may be patients who may prefer revascu-\nlarization even if not on GDMT.\nDownloaded from http://ahajournals.org by on June 6, 2026\n\n	As a highly skilled clinical medical AI, I will answer based on the updated clinical guidelines provided in the context. \nUrinalysis and urine culture are two different diagnostic tests used to evaluate urinary tract infections.\n\nUrinalysis is a laboratory test that examines the physical and chemical properties of urine, such as pH, specific gravity, and the presence of proteins, glucose, and ketones. It also includes microscopic examination of the urine sediment to identify cells, casts, and crystals.\n\nOn the other hand, urine culture is a microbiological test that identifies the presence and type of bacteria or other microorganisms in the urine. It is typically used to diagnose urinary tract infections (UTIs), such as cystitis or pyelonephritis.\n\nIn the context of chronic coronary disease management, urine culture may be used to monitor for kidney infections, which can be a complication of revascularization procedures.\n\nIn summary, urinalysis is a test that evaluates the physical and chemical properties of urine, while urine culture is a test that identifies the presence and type of microorganisms in the urine. (Source: AHA-ACC-ACCP-ASPC-NLA-PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease: A Report of the American Heart Association - American College of Cardiology Joint Committee on Clinical Pr