ng Positive Health Behaviors and Well-being to Improve Health Outcomes: Standards of Care in Diabetes—2024.pdf, Pág: 13) ---\n[0.55–0.69],n = 11) and fewer microvascu-\nlar complications (0.76 [0.67–0.86], n =8 ) .\nDose-response meta-analyses showed that\nphysical activity was associated with lower\nrisk of diabetes-related complications even\nat lower levels (326).\nRetinopathy\nIf proliferative diabetic retinopathy or se-\nvere nonproliferative diabetic retinopathy\nis present, then vigorous-intensity aerobic\nor resistance exercise may be contraindi-\ncated because of the risk of triggering vit-\nreous hemorrhage or retinal detachment\n(327). Consultation with an ophthalmolo-\ngist prior to engaging in an intense exer-\ncise plan may be appropriate.\nPeripheral Neuropathy\nDecreased pain sensation and a higher\npain threshold in the extremities can re-\nsult in an increased risk of skin breakdown,\ninfection, and Charcot joint destruction\nwith some forms of exercise. Therefore, a\nthorough assessment should be done to\nensure that neuropathy does not alter kin-\nesthetic or proprioceptive sensation dur-\n\n	Based on the updated clinical guidelines, the patient's serum creatinine level has decreased from 1.7 mg/dL to 1.2 mg/dL within 1 hour. This improvement suggests that the patient's kidney function is improving. The patient's symptoms of muscle pain and worsening pain in the right leg, along with the significant increase in serum creatine kinase and normal creatinine level, suggest rhabdomyolysis.\n\nRhabdomyolysis is a serious condition characterized by muscle damage that can lead to myoglobinuria (myoglobin in the urine) and potentially acute kidney injury. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management would be to closely monitor the patient's kidney function, including serum creatinine and estimated glomerular filtration rate, to ensure that the kidneys are recovering from the injury and not experiencing further damage. This is in line with the guidelines in Fragmento 1, which recommend monitoring for kidney function in people with diabetes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: (C) Measurement of lower extremity compartment pressures.\n\nExplanation:\nThe option A, application of ice, is not the most appropriate next step because it may worsen the patient's symptoms and potentially exacerbate rhabdomyolysis.\n\nOption B, cyclobenzaprine therapy, is not appropriate because it is a muscle relaxant, and the patient's symptoms are not related to muscle spasms or cramps.\n\nOption C, measurement of lower extremity compartment pressures, is the most appropriate next step because it can help assess the severity of the patient's rhabdomyolysis and help monitor for potential complications, such as compartment syndrome.\n\nOption D, MRI of the right lower extremity, is not the most appropriate next step because it is not necessary to diagnose rhabdomyolysis and can expose the patient to unnecessary radiation.\n\nOption E, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug therapy, is not appropriate because it can worsen kidney damage in the setting of rhabdomyolysis.	2026-06-06 21:51:31.293377
