 06 June 2026\n\n--- Fragmento 3 (Fonte: 10. Cardiovascular Disease and Risk Management: Standards of Care in Diabetes—2024.pdf, Pág: 5) ---\nable for individuals with diabetes and\nmildly elevated blood pressure (systolic\n>120 mmHg or diastolic>80 mmHg) and\nshould be initiated along with pharmaco-\nlogic therapy when hypertension is diag-\nnosed (Fig. 10.2) (53). A lifestyle therapy\nplan should be developed in collaboration\nwith the person with diabetes and dis-\ncussed as part of diabetes management.\nUse of internet or mobile-based digital\nplatforms to reinforce healthy behaviors\nmay be considered as a component of\ncare, as these interventions have been\nfound to enhance the efﬁcacy of medical\ntherapy for hypertension (54,55).\nPharmacologic Interventions\nRecommendations\n10.7 Individuals with conﬁrmed ofﬁce-\nbased blood pressure$130/80 mmHg\nqualify for initiation and titration of\npharmacologic therapy to achieve the\nrecommended blood pressure goal of\n<130/80 mmHg. A\n10.8 Individuals with conﬁrmed ofﬁce-\nbased blood pressure$150/90 mmHg\nshould, in addition to lifestyle therapy,\nhave prompt initiation and timely titra-\n\n	Based on the provided clinical guidelines, the recommended treatment for patients with diabetes and confirmed hypertension depends on their initial blood pressure (BP) and the presence or absence of albuminuria or coronary artery disease (CAD).\n\nIf the initial BP is 130/80 mmHg or lower and there is no albuminuria or CAD, lifestyle management should be initiated along with pharmacologic therapy as part of diabetes management. The patient should be monitored for BP control and adverse effects. If the BP control is not adequate or adverse effects occur, a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist can be considered, or the patient can be referred to a specialist with expertise in BP management.\n\nIf the initial BP is 150/90 mmHg or higher, prompt initiation and timely titration of pharmacologic therapy are necessary. The recommended treatment is a combination of an ACEi or ARB and either a CCB or diuretic. If the treatment is tolerated and the target BP is achieved, no further action is needed. If the target BP is not met or adverse effects occur, one drug from each of three classes (ACEi or ARB, CCB, and diuretic) can be considered.\n\nTherefore, the best hypertension treatment for patients with diabetes involves a combination of lifestyle management, regular monitoring of BP and adverse effects, and appropriate pharmacologic interventions based on the guidelines provided.	2026-06-06 22:02:21.091135
