6 June 2026\n\n--- Fragmento 3 (Fonte: 10. Cardiovascular Disease and Risk Management: Standards of Care in Diabetes—2024.pdf, Pág: 5) ---\nable for individuals with diabetes and\nmildly elevated blood pressure (systolic\n>120 mmHg or diastolic>80 mmHg) and\nshould be initiated along with pharmaco-\nlogic therapy when hypertension is diag-\nnosed (Fig. 10.2) (53). A lifestyle therapy\nplan should be developed in collaboration\nwith the person with diabetes and dis-\ncussed as part of diabetes management.\nUse of internet or mobile-based digital\nplatforms to reinforce healthy behaviors\nmay be considered as a component of\ncare, as these interventions have been\nfound to enhance the efﬁcacy of medical\ntherapy for hypertension (54,55).\nPharmacologic Interventions\nRecommendations\n10.7 Individuals with conﬁrmed ofﬁce-\nbased blood pressure$130/80 mmHg\nqualify for initiation and titration of\npharmacologic therapy to achieve the\nrecommended blood pressure goal of\n<130/80 mmHg. A\n10.8 Individuals with conﬁrmed ofﬁce-\nbased blood pressure$150/90 mmHg\nshould, in addition to lifestyle therapy,\nhave prompt initiation and timely titra-\n\n	The best hypertension treatment for patients with diabetes, according to the provided medical guidelines, should follow these steps:\n\n1. Initial blood pressure reading of ≥130/80 mmH mant to <150/90 mmHg:\n   - If the patient has albuminuria or CAD (coronary artery disease), initiate treatment with a single antihypertensive agent, such as an ACE inhibitor (ACEi) or an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB).\n   - If no albuminuria or CAD is present, start therapy with an ACEi or ARB along with a calcium channel blocker (CCB) or a diuretic.\n\n2. Blood pressure control and adverse effects assessment:\n   - Regularly evaluate the patient's blood pressure and monitor for any adverse effects related to the antihypertensive medication.\n\n3. Lifestyle management and blood pressure goals:\n   - Develop a lifestyle management plan in collaboration with the patient, which may include dietary changes, physical activity, smoking cessation, and weight loss.\n   - Use digital platforms to reinforce healthy behaviors.\n\n4. Titration and specialist referral:\n   - If the patient's blood pressure does not reach the recommended target, consider adding a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist.\n   - Refer the patient to a specialist with expertise in blood pressure management if necessary.\n\n5. Starting a second antihypertensive agent:\n   - If the patient's blood pressure is not controlled (≥150/90 mmHg) with initial therapy, consider adding a second antihypertensive agent from two different classes, such as an ACEi/ARB with a CCB or a CCB with a diuretic.\n\nBy following these recommendations, healthcare providers can ensure effective blood pressure control and minimize the risk of cardiovascular complications in patients with diabetes.	2026-06-06 22:30:17.127989
