orted in the various\nCGM brands’user manuals, additional in-\nterferences have been discovered after the\nmarket release of these products. Hydroxy-\nurea, used for myeloproliferative disorders\nand hematologic conditions, is one of the\nmost recently identiﬁed interfering sub-\n\n	Keratosis Pilaris is a common, harmless skin condition that causes small, hard bumps on the skin. These bumps are often light brown or red and appear mainly on the upper arms, thighs, buttocks, and sometimes on the face. They are caused by a buildup of keratin, a protein that protects the skin from harmful substances and infection.\n\nKeratosis Pilaris is usually found in individuals of all ages, but it is most common in children and adolescents. It often runs in families, and the exact cause of this condition is not fully understood. However, it is believed to be related to overactive hair follicles and skin dryness.\n\nThe bumps from Keratosis Pilaris are typically not painful or itchy, but they may become more noticeable and bothersome as they grow in size or number. Treatment options for Keratosis Pilaris include topical creams, moisturizers, and, in some cases, medicated ointments. These treatments aim to reduce the appearance of the bumps and alleviate any associated itching.\n\nWhile Keratosis Pilaris is not harmful or contagious, some individuals may find the appearance of the bumps bothersome and seek treatment to improve the skin's appearance and texture. It's essential to consult a dermatologist for a proper diagnosis and personalized treatment plan if you suspect you have Keratosis Pilaris.\n\nIMPORTANT NOTE: The provided context does not directly discuss Keratosis Pilaris. The information provided above is based on general knowledge about the condition.\n\n## Instruction:\n\nGiven the following clinical guidelines and patient information, provide an in-depth medical assessment and management plan for the patient, considering the guidelines and information provided. Use your 