eported in the various\nCGM brands’user manuals, additional in-\nterferences have been discovered after the\nmarket release of these products. Hydroxy-\nurea, used for myeloproliferative disorders\nand hematologic conditions, is one of the\nmost recently identiﬁed interfering sub-\n\n	Keratosis pilaris is a common, benign condition characterized by the appearance of small, rough, and sometimes inflamed bumps on the skin. It is caused by the buildup of keratin, a protein found in skin cells, on the skin's surface. \n\nThe condition is most commonly found on the arms, legs, buttocks, and cheeks, and can be caused by a variety of factors, including:\n\n* Genetic predisposition: Keratosis pilaris is more common in people with a family history of the condition.\n* Dry skin: Dry skin can increase the likelihood of keratosis pilaris.\n* Skin conditions: Certain skin conditions, such as eczema, psoriasis, and atopic dermatitis, can increase the risk of developing keratosis pilaris.\n* Nutritional deficiencies: Deficiencies in vitamins such as B12 and iron, as well as a diet low in omega-3 fatty acids, may contribute to the development of keratosis pilaris.\n* Hormonal changes: Hormonal fluctuations during pregnancy, menopause, or puberty can also contribute to the development of keratosis pilaris.\n\nSymptoms of keratosis pilaris include:\n\n* Small, rough, and sometimes inflamed bumps on the skin\n* Dry, rough, or scaly skin\n* Itching or tingling sensations\n\nTreatment for keratosis pilaris typically involves:\n\n* Moisturizing: Keeping the skin well-moisturized can help to reduce the appearance of keratosis pilaris.\n* Exfoliating: Gently exfoliating the skin with a loofah or exfoliating gloves can help to remove dead skin cells and reduce the appearance of keratosis pilaris.\n* Topical treatments: Topical creams or ointments, such as urea or glycolic acid, can be used to help soften and remove dead skin cells.\n* Avoiding irritants: Avoiding irritants, such as harsh soaps or exfoliants, can help to prevent exacerbating keratosis pilaris.\n\nIt is important to note that keratosis pilaris is a benign condition and is not a sign of any underlying disease. However, if you experience symptoms of keratosis pilaris, it is recommended to consult with a dermatologist or other healthcare professional for a proper diagnosis and treatment plan.	2026-06-06 23:14:21.531963
