t, addition of a \nsecond antianginal agent from a different thera-\npeutic class (beta blockers, CCB, long-acting \nnitrates) is recommended for relief of angina or \nequivalent symptoms.*3–6\n1 B-R\n 3. In patients with CCD, ranolazine is recommended \nin patients who remain symptomatic despite  \ntreatment with beta blockers, CCB, or long- \nacting nitrate therapies.*7, 8\n1 B-NR\n 4. In patients with CCD, sublingual nitroglycerin or \nnitroglycerin spray is recommended for immediate \nshort-term relief of angina or equivalent symp-\ntoms.*9,10\n3: Harm B-R\n 5. In patients with CCD and normal L V function, the \naddition of ivabradine to standard anti-anginal \ntherapy is potentially harmful.*11\n\n	D. Topiramate\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe patient presents with symptoms consistent with trigeminal neuralgia (also known as tic douloureux). The most common medications used to treat trigeminal neuralgia are anticonvulsants. According to the context provided in Fragmento 1, the available data for managing older patients with chronic coronary disease (CCD) are limited. Therefore, the context does not provide enough information to determine which antianginal therapy is most appropriate for this patient. However, the patient's symptoms do not align with CCD, and there is no mention of angina or equivalent symptoms. Instead, the patient's symptoms are suggestive of trigeminal neuralgia. The context in Fragmento 2 lists Topiramate as a medication for weight management. However, Topiramate is also an approved drug for the treatment of trigeminal neuralgia. Therefore, based on the patient's symptoms and the available information, the most appropriate pharmacotherapy at this time is Topiramate (Option D).	2026-06-06 23:37:26.26084
