ocused up-\ndate of the 2014 AHA/ACC/HRS guideline for the management of pa-\ntients with atrial fibrillation: a report of the American College of Cardiology/\nAmerican Heart Association Task Force on Clinical Practice Guidelines and \nthe Heart Rhythm Society. Circulation. 2019;140:e125–e151.\n 17 . Otto CM, Nishimura RA, Bonow RO, et al. 2020 ACC/AHA guideline for the \nmanagement of patients with valvular heart disease: a report of the Ameri-\ncan College of Cardiology/American Heart Association Joint Committee on \nClinical Practice Guidelines. Circulation. 2021;143:e72–e227 .\nDownloaded from http://ahajournals.org by on June 6, 2026\n\n	B. Cor pulmonale\n\nEXPLANATION:\nBased on the clinical guidelines context provided, the patient's cardiac examination reveals a grade 3/6 systolic murmur, left ventricular hypertrophy on ECG, mild cardiomegaly with left ventricular prominence on chest x-ray, and weak femoral pulses with a radiofemoral delay. These findings suggest the possibility of chronic left-sided heart disease, such as chronic mitral stenosis or left ventricular dysfunction. Cor pulmonale is a complication of chronic left-sided heart disease, where the right ventricle hypertrophies and fails due to increased pressure from the left ventricle. This condition is not directly related to atrial fibrillation, systemic hypertension, or tricuspid valve regurgitation. Therefore, the correct answer is B.	2026-06-06 23:41:41.413122
