tients \nwith stable ischemic heart disease: a report of the American College of \nCardiology Foundation/American Heart Association Task Force on Practice \nGuidelines, and the American College of Physicians, American Association \nfor Thoracic Surgery, Preventive Cardiovascular Nurses Association, Society \nfor Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions, and Society of Thoracic\n\n--- Fragmento 2 (Fonte: 2023 AHA-ACC-ACCP-ASPC-NLA-PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease: A Report of the American Heart Association - American College of Cardiology Joint Committee on Clinical Practice Guidelines.pdf, Pág: 74) ---\nAssociation Task Force on Performance Measures and Task Force on Prac-\ntice Guidelines. Circulation. 2014;129:2329–2345.\n 2. World Health Organization. CHOosing Interventions that are Cost Effective \n(WHO-CHOICE): cost-effectiveness thresholds. Accessed June 3, 2022. \nhttp://www.who.int/choice/en/.\n 3. Fihn SD, Gardin JM, Abrams J, et al. 2012 ACCF/AHA/ACP/AATS/\nPCNA/SCAI/STS guideline for the diagnosis and management of patients \nwith stable ischemic heart disease: a report of the American College of \nCardiology Foundation/American Heart Association Task Force on Practice \nGuidelines, and the American College of Physicians, American Association \nfor Thoracic Surgery, Preventive Cardiovascular Nurses Association, Society \nfor Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions, and Society of Thoracic \nSurgeons. Circulation. 2012;126:e354–e471.\n 4. Fihn SD, Blankenship JC, Alexander KP, et al. 2014 ACC/AHA/AATS/\nPCNA/SCAI/STS focused update of the guideline for the diagnosis and\n\n--- Fragmento 3 (Fonte: 2023 AHA-ACC-ACCP-ASPC-NLA-PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease: A Report of the American Heart Association - American College of Cardiology Joint Committee on Clinical Practice Guidelines.pdf, Pág: 75) ---\ncan Society of Hypertension, American Society of Nephrology, Association \nof Black Cardiologists, and European Society of Hypertension. Circulation. \n2011;123:2434–2506.\n 33. Patel MR, Bailey SR, Bonow RO, et al. ACCF/SCAI/AATS/AHA/ASE/\nASNC/HFSA/HRS/SCCM/SCCT/SCMR/STS 2012 appropriate use \ncriteria for diagnostic catheterization: a report of the American College of \nCardiology Foundation Appropriate Use Criteria Task Force, Society for \nCardiovascular Angiography and Interventions, American Association for \nThoracic Surgery, American Heart Association, American Society of Echo-\ncardiography, American Society of Nuclear Cardiology, Heart Failure Society \nof America, Heart Rhythm Society, Society of Critical Care Medicine, So-\nciety of Cardiovascular Computed Tomography, Society for Cardiovascular \nMagnetic Resonance, and Society of Thoracic Surgeons. J Am Coll Cardiol. \n2012;59:1995–2027 .\n 34. Barua RS, Rigotti NA, Benowitz NL, et al. 2018 ACC expert consensus\n\n	C. Pain reduction\n\nEXPLANATION:\nGiven the patient's history of thoracotomy and the presence of mild erythema at the incision site, it is reasonable to assume that the patient is experiencing pain. The most appropriate next step in management would be pain reduction. The other options are not indicated based on the provided clinical information.\n\nA. Administration of broad-spectrum antibiotics: Although there is a risk of infection after thoracic surgery, the patient's vital signs and oxygen saturation are stable, and there are no signs of infection such as fever or leukocytosis. Therefore, antibiotics are not necessary at this time.\n\nB. Initiation of tube feedings: The patient is not showing signs of nutritional deficiency, and there is no indication of gastrointestinal surgery or bowel obstruction. Tube feedings are not required at this stage of the patient's care.\n\nD. Physical therapy: Physical therapy is generally used to improve mobility, strength, and function in patients with injuries or diseases that limit their ability to move. In this case, the patient's decreased respiratory excursion is most likely due to the surgery itself, and physical therapy would not be the most appropriate next step in management.\n\nE. Psychological evaluation for post-traumatic stress disorder: PTSD is a psychiatric condition that can develop after experiencing or witnessing a traumatic event. While it is important to consider the psychological well-being of patients, there is no indication in the patient's history or examination results that suggest he is experiencing PTSD. Therefore, a psychological evaluation is not the most appropriate next step in management at this time.	2026-06-06 23:51:00.204389
