ting possible harm when \nivabradine is used in this setting.\n4.3.7. Management of Refractory Angina\nRecommendation for Management of Refractory Angina\nReferenced studies that support the recommendation are  \nsummarized in the Online Data Supplement. \nCOR LOE Recommendation \n2b B-R\n 1. In patients with CCD, refractory angina, and no \nother treatment options, enhanced external coun-\nterpulsation may be considered for relief of symp-\ntoms.*1\n*Modified from the 2012 ACC/AHA Multisociety Guideline for the Diagnosis \nand Management of Patients With SIHD.2\nSynopsis\nRefractory angina represents a small but important seg-\nment of the CCD population. 3 Patients are identified by \n3 major features: (1) anginal chest pain (or equivalent \nsymptoms) that is uncontrolled despite intensive medi-\ncal therapy; (2) objective evidence the anginal symptoms \nhave an ischemic cause; and (3) no further options for \ncoronary revascularization.4 T ypically, symptoms produce\n\n	B. Intravenous methylprednisolonethereapy\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe patient presents with symptoms of sore throat, fever, change in voice, increased pain with swallowing, and trismus, suggestive of pharyngitis and possibly a complication known as retropharyngeal abscess. According to the updated clinical guidelines, in patients with refractory angina and no other treatment options, enhanced external counterpulsation may be considered for relief of symptoms (Fragmento 3, 2023 AHA-ACC-ACCP-ASPC-NLA-PCNA Guideline). However, the patient in question is presenting with a completely different condition, and the most appropriate management for her symptoms would be intravenous methylprednisolone therapy. The reason is that the patient's symptoms are indicative of a potentially severe infection, which may require anti-inflammatory medication to reduce swelling and inflammation, as well as antibiotic therapy to treat the underlying infection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is (B) Intravenous methylprednisolon