Practice Guidelines. Circulation. 2021;143:e72–e227 .\nDownloaded from http://ahajournals.org by on June 6, 2026\n\n--- Fragmento 2 (Fonte: 2023 AHA-ACC-ACCP-ASPC-NLA-PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease: A Report of the American Heart Association - American College of Cardiology Joint Committee on Clinical Practice Guidelines.pdf, Pág: 93) ---\nAcute Coronary Syndrome or Percutaneous Coronary Intervention: Insights \nFrom AUGUSTUS. Circulation. 2020;141:1618–1627 .\n 64. Lamberts M, Gislason GH, Lip GYH, et al. Antiplatelet therapy for stable \ncoronary artery disease in atrial fibrillation patients taking an oral anticoagu-\nlant. Circulation. 2014;129:1577–1585.\n 65. Lemesle G, Ducrocq G, Elbez Y, et al. Vitamin K antagonists with or without \nlong-term antiplatelet therapy in outpatients with stable coronary artery dis-\nease and atrial fibrillation: association with ischemic and bleeding events. \nClin Cardiol. 2017;40:932–939.\n 66. Bainey KR, Welsh RC, Connolly SJ, et al. Rivaroxaban plus aspirin versus aspirin \nalone in patients with prior percutaneous coronary intervention (COMPASS-\nPCI). Circulation. 2020;14:1141-1151.\n 67 . Hurlen M, Abdelnoor M, Smith P, et al. Warfarin, aspirin, or both after myo-\ncardial infarction. N Engl J Med. 2002;347:969–974.\n\n--- Fragmento 3 (Fonte: 2023 AHA-ACC-ACCP-ASPC-NLA-PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease: A Report of the American Heart Association - American College of Cardiology Joint Committee on Clinical Practice Guidelines.pdf, Pág: 94) ---\nmanagement of heart failure: a report of the American College of Cardiol-\nogy Foundation/American Heart Association Task Force on Practice Guide-\nlines. Circulation. 2013;128:e240–e327 .\n 27 . Sorbets E, Steg PG, Young R, et al. Beta-blockers, calcium antagonists, and \nmortality in stable coronary artery disease: an international cohort study. Eur \nHeart J. 2019;40:1399–1407 .\n 28. Al-Khatib SM, Stevenson WG, Ackerman MJ, et al. 2017 AHA/ACC/HRS \nguideline for management of patients with ventricular arrhythmias and the \nprevention of sudden cardiac death: a report of the American College of \nCardiology/American Heart Association Task Force on Clinical Practice \nGuidelines and the Heart Rhythm Society. Circulation. 2018;138:e272–\ne391.\n 29. January CT, Wann LS, Calkins H, et al. 2019 AHA/ACC/HRS focused up-\ndate of the 2014 AHA/ACC/HRS guideline for the management of pa-\ntients with atrial fibrillation: a report of the American College of Cardiology/\n\n	A. Adenosine\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe patient presents with atrial fibrillation, which is a cardiac arrhythmia characterized by irregular and rapid atrial activation and disorganized atrial contractions. The patient's pulse rate is still high at 160/min, despite previously receiving metoprolol. Adenosine is a short-acting antiarrhythmic medication that can be used to convert atrial fibrillation to sinus rhythm. Adenosine works by acting on the A1 adenosine receptors in the heart, which can slow down the heart rate and increase the conductivity of the atrioventricular (AV) node. Therefore, it is the best option to decrease the patient's pulse rate in this scenario. The other options, digoxin, enalapril, and lidocaine, are not indicated for this specific situation. Digoxin is a cardiac glycoside that can be used to slow down the heart rate, but it is not the most effective or safe option in this case, as the patient is already in atrial fibrillation and has a normal blood pressure. Enalapril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor used for the treatment of heart failure and hypertension, and it is not indicated for the rapid control of heart rate in this patient. Lidocaine is a class Ib antiarrhythmic medication used to treat ventricular arrhythmias, and it is not indicated for atrial fibrillation.	2026-06-07 00:25:02.90225
