ative U waves\nCoronary artery spasm: defined as transient total or subtotal  \ncoronary artery occlusion (>90% constriction) with angina and ischemic \nelectrocardiographic changes either spontaneously or in response to  \na provocative stimulus (typically acetylcholine, ergot, or hyperventilation)\n“Definitive” vasospastic angina is diagnosed if nitrate-responsive angina is \nevident during spontaneous episodes and either the transient ischemic ECG \nchanged during the spontaneous episodes or coronary artery spasm criteria \nare fulfilled. “Suspected” vasospastic angina is diagnosed if nitrate-responsive \nangina is evident during spontaneous episodes but transient ischemic electro-\ncardiographic changes are equivocal or unavailable and coronary artery spasm \ncriteria are equivocal.\nECG indicates electrocardiogram.\n\n	C\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe patient's clinical presentation is consistent with an intrauterine pregnancy (as evidenced by the positive urine pregnancy test, abnormal menstrual history, and ultrasonographic findings of a thickened endometrial lining). However, the presence of vaginal spotting and right lower quadrant pain raises concern for possible complications, such as an ectopic pregnancy or early intrauterine pregnancy-related complications.\n\nDilatation and curettage (D&C) is the most appropriate next step in management to evaluate the intrauterine pregnancy further and assess for any complications. A D&C would allow for direct visualization and sampling of the endometrium, helping to confirm the location and viability of the intrauterine pregnancy and to exclude any potential complications.\n\nOption (A) Admission to the hospital for observation may be appropriate if there were signs of significant hemodynamic instability or other concerning findings, but the patient's vital signs are stable.\n\nOption (B) Diagnostic laparoscopy is typically reserved for cases where there is a strong suspicion of ectopic pregnancy or other intra-abdominal pathology not evident on ultrasonography. While not entirely ruled out, the ultrasound findings suggest an intrauterine pregnancy, making this option less appropriate as an initial step.\n\nOption (D) Follow-up pelvic ultrasonography in 1 week would be premature, as the patient is currently symptomatic, and immediate further evaluation is necessary.\n\nOption (E) Serial serum β-hCG concentrations may be used in certain clinical scenarios, such as monitoring a known pregnancy to assess for intrauterine or ectopic pregnancy, but in this case, the patient is already diagnosed with a positive urine pregnancy test and an ultrasound finding of a thickened endometrial lining. D&C would be the most appropriate next step to assess the intrauterine pregnancy further.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.	2026-06-07 11:16:42.514759
