 meta-\nbolic equivalents. 5 If a patient with CCD can reach this \nlevel during exercise testing without ischemia or symp-\ntoms, then the risk for ischemia during sexual activity is \nlow, especially considering the short exposure period. 2 \nIt is rare for a patient to die from cardiac disease dur-\ning sexual intercourse; in this regard, men appear more \nat risk than women, 6 with the absolute rate being very \nsmall. Sexual activity is associated with 1% of all MIs. 3 \nMen and women with CCD and its risk factors have a \nhigh prevalence of sexual dysfunction. 7 Recent MI and \ncoronary artery bypass surgery may additionally compro-\nmise sexual function 5; in this regard, sexual counselling \nmay be helpful, and resuming sexual activity does not \nappear to be associated with an increased risk of death.\n\n	- [ai]: (B) Hypertension\n\nExplanation: The most critical factor in formulating a management plan for this patient is her hypertension. While the patient's age, obesity, and future fertility plans are relevant factors, the presence of hypertension is particularly concerning in the context of her symptoms and medical history. Hypertension can be a risk factor for various complications and can influence the management plan for her current condition, which appears to be related to a cervical mass possibly indicative of a serious underlying pathology. In this case, hypertension may also increase the risk of cardiovascular complications during any potential surgical intervention. Therefore, it is essential to address and manage her hypertension before considering further diagnostic procedures or treatment options for her cervical mass.\n\nThe stage of disease (E) is also a critical factor, but since the question specifically asks for the most critical factor, hypertension takes precedence due to its systemic implications and potential to affect the entire management plan. The stage of the disease would be assessed through further diagnostic tests and evaluations, which would then inform the appropriate management strategy.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is (B) Hypertension.\n\n---------	2026-06-07 11:27:38.091621
